## First, the pasted text has defects from the patch application

Before the suggestions, four things in what you pasted:

1. **The Compile Header still reads `perfection v1.1`.** §13 explicitly justifies that row: *"§16.2(a) recompile trigger is unobservable without it."* You've changed normative text under §7 and left the version claiming otherwise — every artifact compiled from this build now carries a false recompile signal. Bump to v1.2 in §13 and in the `<perfection>` tag.
2. **`A user whointends`** — missing space.
3. **7.6's indentation is now 3 spaces where 7.1–7.5 and 7.7 use 4**, and the line breaks after `no substitution` and mid-`who intends` split sentences awkwardly. Cosmetic elsewhere; less so in a document that elsewhere demands verbatim emission.
4. **Closing the check I flagged last turn:** §7.2's text is the bare line *"Conflict precedence when admissible sources disagree."* No distinct-tier presumption. That clears — no follow-on edit needed.

One substantive point on the patch itself: since 7.6's default is *no substitution*, the `SCOPE OF SUBSTITUTION` clause has no work to do unless the user enables substitution. Under §16.1 ("ships the specific rule, never the section"), it should ship conditionally — only when substitution is actually configured. Otherwise every artifact carries a rule about a mechanism that isn't active.

## The suggestions, one at a time

**1. Domain Profiles — applies, but not as a drop-in. It collides with §3.3.**

The reviewer is right that there's a real gap, but wrong about where. §3.3 requires that when an answer fails to resolve an item you *decompose* it, offering "2–4 concrete candidate answers **drawn from the user's own supplied material** (elicitation devices requiring selection, never invented as business fact)." When an item is unresolved *precisely because the user supplied nothing on it*, there is no material to draw from, and §3.3's decomposition route is unavailable. That's a latent hole, and profiles do patch it.

But a profile's candidates are by definition not from user material. So profiles cannot be added without amending §3.3's parenthetical — otherwise they are a §3.3 violation on their face. The safe formulation: profile contributions enter exclusively as `<proposal>` items under §6.4, carry no authority until ratified, and may never satisfy a Tier-1 gate. Note also that profiles deliver **zero** reduction in Tier-1 questioning, because §3.2 forbids defaulting Tier 1 under any circumstance. The gain is question *quality*, not question *count* — which is worth having, but is a smaller claim than "increases usability dramatically."

The reviewer's own example is the trap: `Reviewer: Senior Editor` as a profile default. §6.3 requires role *plus* 2–4 named refusals *plus* decision authority; a bare job title is explicitly insufficient. And reviewer precedence "may never be defaulted or inferred from seniority" (§6.3, §17.7). A profile that pre-fills reviewers is a §9 laundering vector.

**2. Modularization — the diagnosis is right, the remedy is wrong.**

"Sections increasingly interact" is correct — you and I just spent a turn on exactly that, a scoping rule in 7.6 that silently failed to constrain 7.2. But splitting into Module A/B/C/D does not reduce semantic coupling; it *hides* it behind a file boundary, and the invariants that matter (source tier ↔ conflict precedence ↔ reviewer precedence ↔ tiering) are precisely the ones that cross module lines.

The costs are also concrete. §16.1 declares its section enumeration **exhaustive**. §10 has a CROSS-REFERENCE check that fails "if any §n reference resolves to a wrong or absent section." Renumbering touches every cross-reference in the document and both of those mechanisms. High risk, invisible to users, no correctness gain.

What would actually address the diagnosis: a short **cross-section invariant registry** — an explicit list of the couplings that must hold (7.6→7.2 scope, 6.1 tiering→6.3 trigger, 4→6.1 blocking values, 15.2→10 provenance). One screen, no renumbering, and it makes the next 7.6-class bug findable before it ships. Recommend that instead.

**3. Design Principles — applies, conditionally.**

Harmless and genuinely useful for onboarding, *if* marked non-normative. The risk is specific: §9 is precedence-bearing, and a vaguer restatement of §9 near the top of the document creates two texts that can be cited against each other. Note that this compiler has **no rule for resolving conflicts between its own sections** — §6.1 and §8.1 have local "first match wins" ladders, but §9's precedence governs compiled-prompt-vs-runtime-input, not compiler-internal disputes. Adding a principles section without also adding "principles are non-normative and resolve nothing; the numbered rule governs" would introduce the ambiguity class you just paid to remove from 7.6.

**4. Ownership Model — largely already present.**

§6.3 already does this: precedence "**scoped by decision domain where the user supplies one** (e.g. content decisions to the editor, policy decisions to compliance, regulatory interpretation to legal)," and §17.7 demonstrates it end to end. The reviewer appears to have missed it.

The real gap is narrower and is the *same bug class as 7.6*: if the user supplies domain scoping that is **incomplete or overlapping**, §6.3 has no rule. A decision falling in no reviewer's domain, or in two, has no resolution, and the REVIEWER_UNDEFINED gate doesn't fire because *a* precedence structure was supplied. Worth patching:

```text
- RESIDUAL PRECEDENCE: where precedence is scoped by decision domain, the
  specification must also state a residual order governing decisions that fall
  in no named domain or in two. Domain scoping does not satisfy this section
  without it. Absent a residual order, gate it, error_code REVIEWER_UNDEFINED.
  Residual precedence is TIER 1.
```

**5. Source Mode NONE/LIGHT/STRICT — mostly redundant, and LIGHT is unsafe.**

NONE already exists: §7 opens *"Applies whenever the task involves external factual claims,"* and §13 omits the section when inapplicable. The memo-rewrite example already costs nothing.

LIGHT cannot be built. 7.1, 7.2 and 7.5 are Tier 1; "simple provenance requirements" means defaulting them, which §3.2 forbids outright. A mode that gates rather than asks them is just `/fast`, which already exists.

The overstatement: 7.3, 7.4, 7.6 and 7.7 are *already* Tier 2 and defaultable with disclosure, and §3.3 caps questions at 3/turn with Tier 1 first — so §7's questioning load is already bounded. "Reduce unnecessary questioning" is solving a problem the tier system solved.

The one genuine defect here: **§7's applicability test has no ambiguity rule**, unlike §6.1 rule 4. Nothing says who decides whether a task "involves external factual claims," or what happens when that's unclear. One line fixes it: *"If applicability is unclear, §7 applies (per §3.2's ambiguous tiering rule)."*

## On "the bottleneck is no longer correctness"

I'd reject that. This turn alone surfaced a live version-row falsehood, an unbounded residual-precedence hole in §6.3, a missing ambiguity rule in §7, and an unavailable-decomposition path in §3.3. The reviewer's own top recommendation is unimplementable as written because it contradicts §3.3. Correctness work isn't finished; it has just moved from single-section rules to cross-section invariants — which is an argument for the invariant registry, not for modules.

Their "not more validation rules / error codes / passes" calls are all correct, and for the right reasons.

Also: S-tier item 2, "Architecture Reviewer prompt," appears nowhere in the body of the suggestions. I can't evaluate it. If you want it assessed, say what it is.

**If you want any of the four endorsed patches applied** — v1.2 header bump, 7.6 typo/indent cleanup, §6.3 residual precedence, §7 applicability ambiguity — paste v1.1 and I'll return the full v1.2 with exactly those changes and nothing else touched. Same constraint as before: I won't reconstruct the document from my own citations to it.
